<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garegin Nzhdeh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Garegin Nzhdeh was an Armenian military commander who defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a> against Azerbaijani and Bolshevik forces in 1920–1921 and led the Republic of Mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. His resistance helped secure Zangezur for Armenia before he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Captured by the Soviets in 1944, he died in Vladimir prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh.jpg" alt="Garegin Nzhdeh" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garegin_Nzhdeh_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Garegin Ter-Harutyunyan, known by his nom de guerre Nzhdeh, was born on 1 January 1886 in the village of Kznut in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a>, the youngest of four children of a village priest. He studied at a Russian school in Nakhichevan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> and for two years at the law faculty of Saint Petersburg Imperial University, then returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> to join the Armenian national movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In 1906 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and in 1907 was commissioned lieutenant in the Bulgarian army. In 1908 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After three years in a Russian prison from 1909, Nzhdeh joined General Andranik Ozanian in 1912 in an Armenian battalion of the Bulgarian army in the Balkan wars. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served in Armenian volunteer units of the Russian army, and in May 1918 he organised the defence of Karakilisa against Ottoman forces. In 1920, while stationed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, he expelled the Turkic-speaking inhabitants of several settlements.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> became Soviet on 2 December 1920 he rejected the proposal to place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a> in Soviet Azerbaijan and declared Syunik self-governing. The 2nd All-Zangezur Congress declared the Republic of Mountainous Armenia on 26 April 1921, with Nzhdeh as prime minister and minister of defence. After months of fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, it capitulated in July 1921 on Soviet promises to keep the region in Soviet Armenia, and Nzhdeh crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, quarrelled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a> over relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and in 1933 founded the youth movement Tseghakron in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the Second World War Nzhdeh proposed supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> if they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and in 1942 he joined the Armenian National Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, which Nazi Germany created to coerce Armenian prisoners of war into joining its Armenian Legion. He was arrested in 1944 and on 24 April 1948 was sentenced to 25 years of imprisonment for counterrevolutionary activities of 1920 and 1921. He died in Vladimir Central Prison on 21 December 1955. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s prosecutor general rehabilitated him on 30 March 1992, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> widely admire him as a national hero. His wartime record has drawn criticism, including from Israel's ambassador in January 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Garegin Nzhdeh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Red Army suppressed the revolt of February 1921, and the anti-Soviet rebellion of <b>Garegin Nzhdeh</b> in mountainous Zangezur was defeated, its leaders driven across the Araks River into Iran on 15 July 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Armenian SSR replaced the First Republic of Armenia in December 1920, the southern mountain district of Zangezur, defended by the commander <b>Garegin Nzhdeh</b>, refused to accept Soviet rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mountainous-armenia">Republic of Mountainous Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Güznüt, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in Vladimir Central Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.13,45.54&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Güznüt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Garegin Nzhdeh"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4051 4162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4051 4162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.13,45.54&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
